- op: discuss  # 23fd
  at: '2026-03-04T09:35:46Z'  # 23fd
  by: agent  # 23fd
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 23fd
  clock: 12  # 23fd
  nonce: 23fd  # 23fd
  message: "Good question. The cross-analyzer field-type-resolution infrastructure we just built (PR #1731) is exactly the kind of shared infrastructure that benefits multiple analyzers. The base class now provides: (1) FileAnalysis.class_field_types for subclasses to populate, (2) automatic aggregation into _field_type_registry between passes, (3) resolve_receiver_type() helper for walking self.field chains. This is already used by Rust and can be adopted by C#, C++, Swift (tracker items WI-pufaj, WI-puhod, WI-risan). For the remaining Rust-specific gap (trait-aware disambiguation for non-field method calls), the question is whether a shared trait resolution mechanism would benefit other analyzers. Likely not worth shared infrastructure — trait dispatch is Rust-specific (Go interfaces resolved differently, Java/C# interfaces already have type annotations). Recommendation: the field-type-registry infrastructure was worth building (cross-language benefit). Rust trait-aware resolution should stay Rust-specific (low cross-language benefit)."  # 23fd
